  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting significant education policy reforms in the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM) through the Improving Quality of Basic Education (IQBE) project co-funded and implemented by the Asian Development Bank. With support from IQBE, the National Working Group for Teacher Development was established, and through the working group the first FSM National Professional Learning and Development Framework was developed.  The framework which directly addresses Goal 2 of FSM&#x2019;s Education Sector Strategic Development Plan was endorsed on date by the FSM Association of Chief State School Officers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia is supporting the FSM in creating professionals and leaders through the Australia Awards scholarship programs by achieving higher education degrees for FSM citizens.
&#x200d;
A historic achievement for the FSM, was the first graduate to earn a doctorate degree under the Australia Awards Scholarship was recorded. From Chuuk state, Myjolynne Kim received a Doctor of Philosophy in Culture, History, and Language from the ANU in February 2025. She currently serves as the Deputy Chief of Mission at the FSM Embassy in Australia, under the leadership of Ambassador Jane Chigiyal, strengthening ties between FSM and Australia. Her dedication and perseverance sets an example for future scholars.
&#x200d;
FSM alumni are taking up leadership roles in their professions across key sectors. Remarkably in the health sector, four (4) of our alumni who received their degrees in Bachelor of Medicine and Bachelor of Surgery (MBBS) at the Fiji National University (FNU) under the Australia Awards Pacific Scholarships are now serving in state hospitals.
&#x200d;
Other alumni are now serving both public and private sectors in law and public administration, cyber security field, engineering, commerce, and tourism.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In partnership with UNICEF through the Wash in Schools (WinS) project, Australia supported COVID-19 response efforts through education and by improving systems and resources for infection prevention and control (IPC) in the Federated States of Micronesia (FSM).  For example, an IPC map was developed through a survey conducted of 78 FSM schools, providing effective solutions for schools to better respond to the impact of COVID-19. Furthermore, the WinS project also conducted workshops to educate over 300 students, staff, and parents on IPC to allow schools to reopen safely after the outbreak.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Education</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In the 2024-2025 school year, the Federated States of Micronesia Improving the Quality of Basic Education (IQBE) project teams aligned their student assessment reporting systems, enhancing clarity and actionability of data. With support from national data teams and an external consultant, the IQBE projects now jointly report the percentage of students meeting minimum competency on exams, categorized by gender at both national and project levels. This shift from prior reporting methods, which focused on benchmarks rather than individual performance, marks significant progress.
&#x200d;
IQBE schools have made substantial gains in Reading and Math since baseline. In 2024, 54% of Grade 6 students met minimum competency in Reading, and 44% in Math, achieving the largest improvement from baseline. On average, IQBE schools have outperformed national averages in both subjects for the last four years. In Grade 6 Reading, IQBE schools outperformed national schools by 14%. In Math, they exceeded national performance by 12%. While Math remains challenging, IQBE students have shown steady improvement, with 37% meeting minimum competency in Grade 4 Math in 2023-2024. In 2024, female students in IQBE schools outperformed male students in all tested categories, particularly in Grade 6 Reading, where they led by 10 percentage points.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Direct Aid Program supported the community project of Redeem Youth Congregation called &#x201c;Wa-ter Security and Sanitation Facility&#x201d; in Kousapw Pohn Pateleng, Madolenihmw, Pohnpei, Federated States of Micronesia. 
&#x200d;
The project initiated the construction of the sanitation facility and handwashing stations for the Pihs community center, with an estimated population of 200 people, with 50 households on the said village. A huge water tank was installed, which connects to water systems such as the rainwater catchment and a water pump that is connected to a small stream, supplying water to the facility. The location of the sani-tation and handwashing facility is right beside the Pihs community center, which is the main venue for the community events and activities.
&#x200d;
The facility will help the community of Pihs, especially the youth to access safe water, sanitation, and good hygiene practices during community events to prevent the spread of infectious diseases and to promote a sanitary environment, which will lead to a healthier and happier lifestyle. The construction was successfully completed in April 2025.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The FSM Electoral Support Program aims to support the Federated States of Micronesia&apos;s (FSM) National Election Office (NEO) to deliver high quality elections through supporting specific technical and capacity development needs. The project also transitioned NEO&apos;s General Voter Registration System to a hybrid online model.The focus was to build NEO&apos;s long term electoral capacity while the AEC also provided support in the lead-up to FSM&apos;s 2027 General Election (which includes the selection of the FSM President through a congressional vote).
&#x200d;
The first deployment included troubleshooting ongoing issues with the current GVRS and training new NEO staff to effectively be able to use the GVRS. This ensures that devices utilising GVRS are functioning appropriately. The deployment was required to ensure that FSM can update their electoral roll and develop a master list.
&#x200d;
The second deployment provided support in the lead up to the 2025 General Election, ensuring NEO were able to successfully synch data to produce a master list and print the electoral roll, which also allows the states to update their electoral voter lists more easily.
&#x200d;
The third deployment by the AEC provided the opportunity to onboarding and training staff on the new  of the GVRS hybrid system.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The FSM Shipwreck Pollution Mitigation Project was implemented from March 2022 to April 2025 to address environment threats posed by World War II shipwrecks in Chuuk Lagoon. With AUD2.12 million in funding from the Australian Government, the project was delivered through a grant agreement with SPREP and Major Projects Foundation as the contractor to deliver the project aimed to mitigate oil spill risks from these wrecks in the FSM.
&#x200d;
Through a comprehensive technical assessment combining multibeam sonar surveys with high-resolution photogrammetry, the project team surveyed the top 20 priority wrecks. Detailed naval architectural analysis revealed the full scale of the environmental threat. Twelve high-priority wrecks were identified as containing significant quantities of oil.
&#x200d;
The project made substantial progress in strengthening local capacity through multiple initiatives. Staff from the Chuuk Heritage Preservation Office received specialised training in wreck survey techniques through the GIRT Scientific Diver Program, enhancing local capabilities for ongoing monitoring and assessment. The project produced several reports that provide the foundation strategic recommendations. These documents include UXO safety protocols, GIRT Scientific Diver training outcomes, Technical Wreck Assessments, Oil Spill Modelling, a comprehensive Risk Assessment with remediation recommendations, Annual Reports, and a Status Report on the potentially polluting wrecks.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
